/-!
# Generators of the strict transform after blowing up a Fitting ideal

Let `F` be a finite type quasi-coherent module on a scheme. After blowing up the closed
subscheme defined by its `k`-th Fitting ideal, the strict transform of `F` can be generated
locally by at most `k` sections.

## Main definitions

There are no new definitions.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.strictTransformSheaf_isGeneratedByNear` gives local generators for the
  strict transform at every point of the blowup.

## Implementation notes

The closed subscheme is the one defined by `AlgebraicGeometry.fittingIdeal`. The strict
transform of a module is `Stacks.StrictTransform.sheaf` for the identity morphism of the base
scheme, the blowup morphism `AlgebraicGeometry.blowUpToBase` and the exceptional ideal
`AlgebraicGeometry.exceptionalIdeal`; its base-change scheme is canonically isomorphic to the
blowup. Generation by `k` sections near each point expresses generation by at most `k` sections,
since generators may be padded by zero.

The argument is affine-local on the blowup. Writing `G` for the pullback of `F` to the base
change and `Z` for the preimage of the exceptional divisor, the strict transform is
`G / (sections supported on Z)`. Around a point of the blowup choose an affine open `U` on which
the inverse image of the exceptional ideal is principal, generated by `f` — possible by Tag
02OS (the exceptional ideal is an effective Cartier divisor) and Tag 053P (an inverse image of
a locally principal subscheme is locally principal). By Tag 0C3D the `k`-th Fitting ideal of `G`
on `U` is then `(f)`. The `f`-primary component of `Γ(G, U)` consists of sections supported on
`Z`, hence maps to zero in the strict transform, so the sections of the strict transform on `U`
are a quotient of `Γ(G, U) / Γ(G, U)_{(f)}` — and the `k`-th Fitting ideal of that quotient is
the unit ideal, by the local-generation criterion of `fittingIdeal_local_generation_tfae`
applied at every maximal ideal. Tag 0C3F then turns the unit-ideal stalk into generation by `k`
sections.

Only one direction of Tag 07ZP's torsion characterization of supported sections is used, and it
is the direction that needs no quasi-coherence: a section killed by a power of `f` has zero germ
wherever `f` is invertible. It is `mem_supportedSectionsSubmodule_of_pow_smul_eq_zero` below.
Tag 07ZP lives in Chapter 28, and the full characterization is not needed here.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CZP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CZP)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

